window.docContents[117].resolve({"/Stlc/#Type-Systems--Stlc___-The-Simply-Typed-Lambda-Calculus--Typing--Contexts":{"contents":"\n\nEven though whole programs are closed, typechecking an abstraction\nλx:T. t means typechecking its body t with the binder λx stripped\noff — so t itself may be open.Question: What is the type of the term \"X Y\"?Answer: It depends on the types of X and Y!I.e., in order to assign a type to a term, we need to know\nwhat assumptions we should make about the types of its free\nvariables.This leads us to a three-place typing judgment, informally\nwritten Γ ⊢ t ⦂ T, where Γ is a\n\"typing context\" — a mapping from variables to their types.\n\nabbrev Context := PartialMap String Ty\n\n\n\n\nFollowing the usual notation for partial maps, we write\nx ↦ T ; Γ for \"update the partial function Γ so\nthat it maps x to T.\"\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Stlc: The Simply Typed Lambda-Calculus\u0009Typing","header":"5.4.1. Contexts","id":"/Stlc/#Type-Systems--Stlc___-The-Simply-Typed-Lambda-Calculus--Typing--Contexts"},"/Sub/#Type-Systems--Sub___-Subtyping--Concepts--The-Subtype-Relation--Arrows":{"contents":"\n\nSuppose we have functions F and G with these types:F : C → Student\nG : (C→Person) → D\nIs it safe to allow the application G F?Yes.So we want:C→Student  <:  C→Person\nI.e., arrow is covariant in its right-hand argument.Now suppose we have:F : Person → C\nG : (Student→C) → D\nIs it safe to allow the application G F?Again yes.So we want:Person → C  <:  Student → C\nI.e., arrow is contravariant in its left-hand argument.Putting these together...     τ₁ <: σ₁\n     σ₂ <: τ₂\n------------------  (arrow)\nσ₁ → σ₂ <: τ₁ → τ₂\n\n\nSuppose we have  σ <: τ and υ <: δ.  Which of the following\nsubtyping assertions is false?(A) σ×υ <: τ×δ(B) τ→υ <: σ→υ(C) (σ→υ) → (σ×δ)  <:  (σ→υ) → (τ×υ)(D) (τ×υ) → δ  <:  (σ×υ) → δ(E) σ→υ <: σ→δ\n\n(C). Applying the arrow rule to the outer arrows reduces this to\nσ×δ <: τ×υ, which by the prod rule needs δ <: υ — the reverse\nof the given υ <: δ.\n\nSuppose again that we have σ <: τ and υ <: δ.  Which of the\nfollowing is incorrect?(A) (τ→τ)×υ  <: (σ→τ)×δ(B) τ→υ <: σ→δ(C) (σ→υ) → (σ→δ)  <:  (τ→υ) → (τ→δ)(D) (σ→δ) → δ  <:  (τ→υ) → δ(E) σ → (δ→υ) <: σ → (υ→υ)\n\n(C). Applying the arrow rule to the outer arrows requires\nσ→δ <: τ→δ, which by the arrow rule again needs τ <: σ — the\nreverse of the given σ <: τ.\n\n","context":"Type Systems\u0009Sub: Subtyping\u0009Concepts\u0009The Subtype Relation","header":"8.1.4.3. Arrows","id":"/Sub/#Type-Systems--Sub___-Subtyping--Concepts--The-Subtype-Relation--Arrows"}});